Using Lbl2Vec and BERTopic for Semi-Supervised Detection of Professionalism Aspects in a ConstructedResponse Situational Judgment Test

Okan Bulut University of Alberta

Alexander MacIntosh Altus Assessments Inc.

Cole Walsh Altus Assessments Inc.

ABSTRACT
Most medical schools in the United States and Canada evaluate applicants based on both academic achievements and non-cognitive (soft) skills such as professionalism. Commonly, medical school applicants are required to complete Casper—an online constructedresponse situational judgment test developed and administered by Altus Assessments to assess various aspects of professionalism such as ethics, empathy, resilience, and collaboration. Examinees writing Casper are given a set of real-life, hypothetical scenarios and asked to explain how they would handle the situation and their reasoning behind it. Since each Casper scenario focuses on multiple aspects of professionalism, examinees do not have to target any specific aspect in their responses. To ensure the correct use and interpretation of Casper scores, the Altus team needs to reliably evaluate whether examinees’ responses for each scenario address the anticipated aspects of professionalism identified by subject matter experts. In this study, we demonstrate how natural language processing (NLP) methods can be employed to characterize the meaning and context of written responses in relation to different aspects of professionalism. Using the Lbl2Vec and BERTopic algorithms, we performed a semi-supervised analysis of written responses from a large sample of medical school applicants (n = 635,106) by clustering the responses into latent topics based on a set of predefined keywords and labels for each professionalism aspect. Our analysis yielded substantive topics that were mostly aligned with the anticipated professionalism aspects. Furthermore, in terms of topical prevalence, some aspects of professionalism (e.g., self-awareness) appeared to be more dominant than the other aspects in the responses. Overall, our proposed approach delivers a feasible mechanism to identify overlapping topics and aspects to maintain test quality and improve human rater training and monitoring in constructed-response situational judgment tests.
Keywords
professionalism, situational judgment test, Casper, topic modeling, BERTopic, semi-supervised, natural language processing
1. INTRODUCTION
Professionalism in the workplace encompasses the conduct, behavior, and attitude of an individual about every aspect of a particular job. In the context of health professions, professionalism refers to the commitment of physicians to “the health and well-being of individual patients and society through ethical practice, high personal standards of behavior, accountability to the profession and society, physician-led regulation, and maintenance of personal health” [1]. Medical school applicants are often required to demonstrate their competency in those aspects of professionalism before entering a medical program. Casper is an online situational judgment test

(SJT) that many medical schools in the United States and Canada use as part of their admissions process. Examinees writing Casper respond to a set of constructed-response items associated with reallife scenarios (e.g., a hypothetical situation with dilemmas around ethics and conflicts). Then, human raters from various professions review the answers to each scenario and rate them holistically.
The CanMEDS framework describes key aspects of professionalism that health professionals strive towards to meet the health care needs of the patients. These aspects are often identified as priorities in Health Professions Education for current and future healthcare professionals [2]. Aligning with CanMEDS and similar frameworks, Casper focuses on ten aspects of professionalism, including collaboration, problem-solving, ethics, and empathy. In each scenario presented in Casper, multiple aspects of professionalism are measured simultaneously, without putting any restrictions on how examinees can answer the items. That is, examinees do not necessarily have to speak to any specific aspect of professionalism in their responses. Although this feature of Casper enables examinees to freely express their thoughts and ideas about the presented scenarios, it makes the scoring of their responses more difficult for human raters. To ensure the correct use and interpretation of Casper scores, it is essential to investigate how examinee responses address different aspects of interests underlying each scenario.
In this study, we build upon prior research presented in Walsh et al. (2022) to further elaborate on the professionalism aspects underlying Casper. Instead of unsupervised natural language processing (NLP) approaches, we employ two semi-supervised NLP approaches, namely, Lbl2Vec [3] and BERTopic [4], to identify aspects of professionalism in examinees’ responses by incorporating theoretically derived keywords and labels of professionalism into NLP models. We address the following research questions:
1. To what extent do the professionalism aspects identified by subject matter experts align with the aspects extracted from responses?
2. How do the aspects of professionalism vary in examinees’ written responses to the Casper items?
2. RELATED WORK
During the admission process, health and professional programs such as medicine want to ensure that they admit students who are academic possess suitable personal and professional qualities to succeed in the profession. Casper allows applicants to demonstrate their professionalism skills earlier in the admissions process. Previous research has shown that Casper is a reliable assessment and predicts future success in medical school [5, 6]. Also, Casper scores indicated moderate correlations with personal and professional

1

qualities measured by the national licensure exams in Canada three to six years after admission to medical school [5].
Assessing professionalism skills accurately is very important for health sciences and medicine programs because medical professionalism is viewed as the foundation of the contact between healthcare workers and society [7]. However, assessing medical professionalism, or more broadly professionalism, remains a formidable challenge in health-related fields. Professionalism is often viewed as a multidimensional construct because it involves multiple personal characteristics such as empathy and trustworthiness, as well as a strong understanding of disciplines such as ethics and social justice [8]. To measure this construct accurately, researchers and practitioners often use SJTs such as Casper. Previous research evaluating the validity of SJTs (i.e., whether the test measures its intended constructs) suggests that each SJT item is likely to measure several dimensions (i.e., constructs), which makes obtaining validity evidence through factor analytic techniques based on itemlevel scores very difficult [9, 10]. Therefore, “new insights into understanding the constructs assessed by SJTs” are highly needed to ensure the correct use and interpretation of SJTs [11].
In this study, we propose a semi-supervised approach to evaluate the dimensionality of SJTs requiring written responses to constructed-response items. Our semi-supervised (or guided) approach relies on the idea of incorporating domain knowledge into language modeling. Previous research shows that domain knowledge can be incorporated into various NLP methods such as topic modeling and text classification by defining several keywords associated with anticipated topics [12]. Similarly, weights from language models trained on general-domain text (e.g., BERT) can be re-adjusted for domain-specific applications by incorporating domain knowledge [13, 14]. It should be noted that defining domain knowledge for SJTs such as Casper is challenging because SJT items refer to contextualized situation descriptions in a hypothetical scenario [15]. Since the scenarios might be context-dependent, it is necessary to identify a collection of keywords to detect higher-level (i.e., context-independent) dimensions underlying the SJT items. In this study, domain knowledge refers to professionalism aspects underlying Casper, such as empathy, resilience, equity, and ethics. Therefore, we identified a set of keywords associated with each aspect of professionalism to guide our analyses.
3. METHODOLOGY
Two of the most widely used NLP embedding methods in recent years are document embeddings and Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers (BERT). In this study, we use Lbl2vec [3] to create a joint embedding of document and word vectors based on pre-defined topic keywords and a special form of BERT, namely BERTopic [4], to compute sentence-level embeddings based on pre-defined topic labels. To detect the aspects of professionalism underlying Casper, we use response data from a large sample of examinees who responded to 311 unique scenarios. Our methodological approach is described in the next sections.
3.1 Dataset
The dataset consisted of 635,106 anonymized written responses to 311 unique scenarios in Casper. Examinees were primarily medical school applicants in the United States. The demographic summary of the sample is as follows: 47% female, 33% male, and 20% nonbinary, other, or unknown; 35% white, 19% Asian, 8% African American, 8% Hispanic, Latino, or Spanish origin; 8% mixed or other, 22% unknown; 31% aged 20-22, 26% aged 23-25, 10% aged

26-28, 4% aged 29 or above, and 30% unknown. Prior to taking Casper, the examinees were informed about the potential use of their anonymized responses for research and development purposes. In addition, ethics approval was obtained from the first author’s institution before conducting the analysis of anonymized Casper responses for this study (Project ID: Pro00114459).

Based on the content underlying the scenarios, subject matter experts involved in developing Casper categorized the scenarios into one of the ten intended professionalism aspects. Each examinee provided written responses to three questions related to each scenario and received a score between 1 and 9. In this study, we combined examinees’ responses to three questions associated with each scenario as a single text and processed them together. To preprocess the combined responses for further analysis, we first performed a spellcheck procedure using the TextBlob library in Python and corrected spelling mistakes in the responses. Next, we turned each word into lowercase characters and removed the punctuations and non-ASCII characters. Lastly, we removed stopwords (e.g., “the”, “a”, “an”, “in”) using the NLTK (Natural Language Toolkit) library in Python.

