3.3 BERTopic
BERTopic [4] is an algorithm for extracting latent semantic structure (i.e., topics) underlying a large set of documents. BERTopic relies on word and/or document embeddings created based on pretrained models, such as SentenceTransformers. These models are essentially pre-trained transformers such as BERT [16] and RoBERTa [17]. Unlike unsupervised topic modeling methods such as Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA), BERTopic enables researchers

to incorporate a list of keywords or pre-defined topic labels into the topic modeling process to produce more interpretable topics via guided or supervised topic modeling.

Semi-supervised topic modeling with BERTopic consists of three steps. First, document embeddings of different granularity (e.g., word, sentences, or documents) are extracted from a large volume of documents. In this step, seed topic representations (i.e., anticipated topics) can be defined by incorporating keywords associated with each seed topic. Then, embeddings for each seed topic are passed through the document embedder to evaluate the relationship between the documents and seed topics. Second, the dimensionality of embeddings is reduced using uniform manifold approximation and projection (UMAP, [18]) and semantically similar documents are clustered together using HDBSCAN [19]. In the final step, latent topics are extracted and reduced based on class-based term frequency, inverse document frequency (c-TF-IDF). c-TF-IDF is an extension of term frequency-inverse document frequency (TFIDF) where all documents from a particular class (e.g., a topic) are grouped together and TF-IDF scores computed for the words within each group of documents. c-TF-IDF scores for class i can be calculated as follows:

