5. CONCLUSIONS AND FUTURE WORK
SJTs such as Casper are widely used by many organizations and professions to evaluate individuals’ ability to choose the most appropriate action in real-life situations. Most medical schools in the United States and Canada require applicants to participate in the Casper test to demonstrate their understanding of “professionalism”. However, the professionalism construct involves multiple aspects, such as problem-solving, ethics, equity, resilience, and empathy. Therefore, responses to constructed-response items from each Casper scenario are expected to address different aspects of professionalism.
In this study, we used two semi-supervised NLP methods to investigate professionalism aspects underlying examinees’ responses to the Casper items. The proposed methods can analyze large volumes of unlabeled textual data using a list of keywords relevant to predefined or anticipated topics. We first identified a list of potential keywords for each professionalism aspect based on the CanMEDS framework and then incorporated the keywords into Lbl2vec and BERTopic to analyze a large sample of written responses. Our findings showed that there was a moderate level of alignment between the intended aspects identified by subject matter experts and the aspects extracted from the data. The alignment was particularly low

5

for some aspects such as problem-solving and empathy, indicating that the examinees focused on other aspects of professionalism when responding to scenarios focusing on problem-solving and empathy.
Our findings also show that professionalism is a multidimensional, complex construct that involves multiple inter-related skills. Thus, it is challenging to label each Casper scenario with a single, dominant aspect. Although the subject matter experts identified the intended aspects for each scenario, examinees’ responses typically involved three aspects of professionalism, on average. For example, examinees discussed problem-solving and resilience together in their responses to several scenarios (see Figure 3). This finding confirmed the necessity of holistic rating process followed by Altus Assessments in scoring Casper responses. Instead of focusing on a specific aspect, raters review the overall quality of Casper responses and assign a single score to multiple written responses associated with each scenario.
The semi-supervised approach presented in this study will drastically improve Altus Assessments’ quality assurance procedures and enable continuous improvements to the Casper test construction and rating procedures. More specifically, the ability to identify which aspects overlap within applicants’ responses allows Altus to refine future tests to have an improved balance of professionalism aspects. Further, NLP methods such as Lbl2vec and BERTopic can be used to direct rater quality assurance and training (e.g., identifying if a rater shows undue preference towards certain aspects).
Further work is needed to explore the relationship between the aspects underlying examinees’ responses and their holistic scores in Casper. For example, we expect each professionalism aspect to contribute equally to Casper scores. However, speaking to some aspects more frequently might be associated with lower or higher scores in Casper. Therefore, the relationship between the aspect composition of responses and corresponding Casper scores should be investigated systematically using NLP-based applications such as automated scoring.
